@TASK[T006]
GOAL{Propose a methodology to measure meaning preservation in a protolanguage.}
CTX{Protolanguage is a reconstructed ancestral language; meaning preservation refers to semantic consistency between reconstructed forms and their descendant reflexes.}
PROBLEM{No direct empirical access to protolanguage; meaning shifts over time; need a systematic, falsifiable measurement.}
PLAN{1. Select a set of cognate sets with well-attested descendant meanings. 2. Define a semantic distance metric (e.g., based on Swadesh list, semantic field overlap, or feature decomposition). 3. For each cognate set, compute the average semantic distance between reconstructed meaning and each descendant meaning. 4. Aggregate distances across all sets to produce a preservation score (e.g., mean or median distance). 5. Compare against a baseline (e.g., random meaning assignment) to assess significance.}
RISK{Reconstruction bias; incomplete descendant data; metric choice may not capture nuanced semantic change; circularity if reconstruction already assumes meaning stability.}
CHK{Validate metric on known language families with historical records (e.g., Romance) to calibrate.}
OUT{Methodology: cognate-based semantic distance aggregation with baseline comparison.}
NEXT{Apply to a test case (e.g., Proto-Indo-European) and report preservation score.}

