PLAN{1. Select a set of core semantic domains (e.g., kinship, body parts, basic actions). 2. For each domain, compile cognate sets from descendant languages with attested meanings. 3. Reconstruct protolanguage forms and assign a single reconstructed meaning per set. 4. Measure semantic distance between reconstructed meaning and each descendant meaning using a semantic similarity metric (e.g., WordNet path similarity or expert-annotated semantic features). 5. Aggregate scores across all sets to produce a preservation index (e.g., mean similarity). 6. Compare against a baseline (e.g., random meaning assignment) to assess significance.}
RISK{Reconstructed meaning may be biased by comparative method assumptions; semantic similarity metrics may not capture cultural or pragmatic shifts.}
CHK{Validate metric on known language families with historical records (e.g., Romance) to calibrate expected preservation rates.}
OUT{A preservation index (0-1) with confidence intervals, plus a list of low-preservation domains for further analysis.}
NEXT{Apply methodology to a test case (e.g., Proto-Indo-European core vocabulary) and report results.}

